# UI Design Rules

## Design Tokens as Source of Truth

- Define colors, typography, spacing, and shadows as design tokens (CSS custom properties or platform-native tokens)
- Never hardcode palette values in component files
- Token naming: `--color-surface`, `--color-text`, `--space-md`, `--text-body`

## Accessibility (WCAG 2.1 AA Minimum)

- Color contrast ratio >= 4.5:1 for normal text, >= 3:1 for large text
- All interactive elements must be keyboard-navigable (tabindex, focus management)
- Form inputs must have associated labels
- Images must have alt text (decorative images: `alt=""`)
- ARIA landmarks on major layout sections (nav, main, complementary)
- Use `prefers-reduced-motion` for users with motion sensitivity
- Use `prefers-color-scheme` for theme support

## Responsive-First

- Design mobile layout first, then enhance for larger screens
- Breakpoints: 320px (mobile), 768px (tablet), 1024px (desktop), 1440px (wide)
- Test against all 4 breakpoints before marking UI work complete
- No horizontal scrolling at any breakpoint

## Visual Regression Testing

- Any component change requires visual regression verification
- Screenshot key states: default, hover, focus, active, disabled, error, loading, empty
- Test both themes (light/dark) when applicable
- Use Playwright screenshot comparison or Chromatic for component libraries

## Animation Guidelines

- Animate compositor-friendly properties only: `transform`, `opacity`, `clip-path`, `filter` (sparingly)
- Never animate layout properties: width, height, top, left, margin, padding, border
- Duration: 200-300ms for micro-interactions, 300-500ms for transitions
- Easing: prefer `cubic-bezier(0.16, 1, 0.3, 1)` for natural motion
- `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce` disables all non-essential animation

## Design Quality Standards

Before shipping any UI:
- [ ] Clear hierarchy through scale and spacing contrast
- [ ] Intentional rhythm (not uniform padding everywhere)
- [ ] Interactive states feel designed (hover, focus, active, disabled)
- [ ] Typography pairing feels intentional, not default
- [ ] Color used semantically, not just decoratively
- [ ] No generic template-looking UI (cards, gradients, centering)

